# A signal whose value is a list, driven the way a person drives one: a package
# holding a store of cells, a suite beside it, and one `buri` command.
#
# **The claim is that `Signal<[T]>` is a cell like any other.** A cell holds one
# whole value and the runtime is told how wide it is and how to take a reference
# on it (`cli/runtime/lib.rs` §2 rule 4). When that value is itself a list, the
# question "which type is `T`" has two readings that no argument list can tell
# apart — the list the call carries, and the list's element — and both backends
# used to answer with the element. So a `Signal<[Int]>` was stored eight bytes
# wide instead of sixteen and read back empty, and a `Signal<[Account]>` was
# retained by a walk over a type it did not hold.
#
# Three widths, because the wrong answer is wrong in a different direction at
# each: an element narrower than the list value truncates it, one wider reads
# and writes past it, and one holding a counted pointer walks a value that is
# not there.
#
# The first block names no store at all. It is here because the defect this case
# pins reached the *process*: a store built anywhere in the binary could leave
# the allocator holding a corrupted free list, and every block in the suite —
# that one included — then came back as `the run exited -1`.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "a cell whose value is a list holds the list, at three element widths, and through a write"

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/store", "--force"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "pass.txt"
}

# The negative twin. One number of one assertion, and the run fails at the block
# that made it — a suite whose cells could not be read wrongly would be a suite
# that never looked in one.
edit {
  file: "lib/store/test/store.buri"
  replace: "assert.length(store.labels.get(ctx), 2);"
  with: "assert.length(store.labels.get(ctx), 9);"
}

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/store", "--force"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "changed.txt"
}
